WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.080
(audience applauding)
- Alright!

00:00:01.080 --> 00:00:02.568
- Hello!
- Have y'all ever met before?

00:00:02.568 --> 00:00:04.813
- No, no, I've never met!
- Never! Hi!

00:00:04.813 --> 00:00:05.646
- Never met!
- [Kelly] Okay.

00:00:05.646 --> 00:00:06.630
I was raving about you.
- It's an honor!

00:00:06.630 --> 00:00:07.582
- Don't let me down.

00:00:07.582 --> 00:00:08.415
- [Female Guest] (laughs)

00:00:08.415 --> 00:00:10.330
- So, Neil, what is the coolest

00:00:10.330 --> 00:00:12.680
new science thing you've
learned to tell me?

00:00:12.680 --> 00:00:14.570
- I learn cool science things every day,

00:00:14.570 --> 00:00:17.420
and sometimes I have to be re-- (laughs),

00:00:17.420 --> 00:00:18.520
I have to be reminded of them.

00:00:18.520 --> 00:00:20.160
Maybe I knew them long ago.

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:21.180
Like we all knew that we have

00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:24.450
very high common DNA with chimpanzees.

00:00:24.450 --> 00:00:26.520
Like 99% identical.

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:28.110
And you say, "Okay, I get that."

00:00:28.110 --> 00:00:30.492
But do you know we also have 50%

00:00:30.492 --> 00:00:33.432
common DNA with a banana?

00:00:33.432 --> 00:00:34.560
(audience laughing)

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:35.791
- I do love bananas.

00:00:35.791 --> 00:00:38.309
- My son, all he eats is bananas.

00:00:38.309 --> 00:00:39.142
- Well, there you go!

00:00:39.142 --> 00:00:39.980
And I got one other for you.

00:00:39.980 --> 00:00:40.813
You ready?

00:00:40.813 --> 00:00:42.440
If you look at the tree of life,

00:00:42.440 --> 00:00:45.164
the common ancestors between humans

00:00:45.164 --> 00:00:49.930
and mushrooms split after--

00:00:49.930 --> 00:00:51.066
- What kind of mushrooms?

00:00:51.066 --> 00:00:53.120
(audience and guests laughing)

00:00:53.120 --> 00:00:55.010
- After the common ancestor,

00:00:55.010 --> 00:00:58.570
between our common
ancestor and green plants.

00:00:58.570 --> 00:01:03.400
Which means we and mushrooms
are more genetically connected

00:01:03.400 --> 00:01:06.427
than either of us are to green plants.

00:01:06.427 --> 00:01:07.264
- What?

00:01:07.264 --> 00:01:08.097
- I know!

00:01:08.097 --> 00:01:09.430
- We are like fungus sometimes.

00:01:09.430 --> 00:01:10.800
- We are fungus among us, yeah.

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:12.113
- We are like fungus sometimes.

00:01:12.113 --> 00:01:13.795
- And--
- [Female Guest] Interesting!

00:01:13.795 --> 00:01:14.930
- And some people have whole relationships

00:01:14.930 --> 00:01:15.763
with mushrooms.

00:01:15.763 --> 00:01:16.596
(audience laughing)

00:01:16.596 --> 00:01:17.429
You know?

00:01:17.429 --> 00:01:18.262
That's what I'm saying?

00:01:18.262 --> 00:01:19.217
You know what I'm saying?
- Yes, yes, they do.

00:01:19.217 --> 00:01:20.050
- I know those people.
- [Neil] (laughs)

00:01:20.050 --> 00:01:21.920
- And I'm like, "Be around people you love

00:01:21.920 --> 00:01:22.753
and that love you!"

00:01:22.753 --> 00:01:23.586
- So, these are new facts.

00:01:23.586 --> 00:01:24.920
I'm more likely to learn a new fact

00:01:24.920 --> 00:01:27.040
in a science that's not my specialty.

00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:28.479
So, but I love learning.
- Me too.

00:01:28.479 --> 00:01:30.870
If it's out of my specialty with science,

00:01:30.870 --> 00:01:32.333
I'm just totally lost.

00:01:32.333 --> 00:01:34.280
(audience and guests laughing)

00:01:34.280 --> 00:01:36.710
Do you have any questions for Neil?

00:01:36.710 --> 00:01:37.814
- Do I have questions?

00:01:37.814 --> 00:01:39.520
Yes, yes.

00:01:39.520 --> 00:01:42.390
Okay, I've been thinking
about this backstage.

00:01:42.390 --> 00:01:47.163
Okay, if an asteroid
were coming to hit Earth,

00:01:47.163 --> 00:01:48.800
how would we stop it?

00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:50.220
I mean, this is possible, right?

00:01:50.220 --> 00:01:51.053
- Oh, yeah.

00:01:51.053 --> 00:01:52.183
So, we know exactly how to stop it.

00:01:52.183 --> 00:01:53.016
- He knows the answer, right?

00:01:53.016 --> 00:01:53.849
- Well, no, no, of course!

00:01:53.849 --> 00:01:54.682
No, no.

00:01:54.682 --> 00:01:55.790
So, there are two ways you could stop it.

00:01:55.790 --> 00:01:57.460
One of them is the macho way.

00:01:57.460 --> 00:01:58.293
- Blow it up?

00:01:58.293 --> 00:02:00.336
- Blow the sucker out of the sky.

00:02:00.336 --> 00:02:01.169
That's the answer.

00:02:01.169 --> 00:02:02.070
- I'm from the South

00:02:02.070 --> 00:02:03.049
- [Neil] Yeah.

00:02:03.049 --> 00:02:04.050
(audience and guests laughing)

00:02:04.050 --> 00:02:05.700
You-- (mumbles comically)
- [Kelly] Just shoot it!

00:02:05.700 --> 00:02:08.040
- Yeah, shoot the sucker out of the sky!

00:02:08.040 --> 00:02:09.264
- Who cares what the ramifications

00:02:09.264 --> 00:02:10.520
for the rest of the universe are!

00:02:10.520 --> 00:02:11.624
- This is what I'm saying!

00:02:11.624 --> 00:02:14.291
In America, we know how to blow stuff up!

00:02:14.291 --> 00:02:17.050
But we don't always know
where the pieces go!

00:02:17.050 --> 00:02:17.883
'Cause if you blow it up,

00:02:17.883 --> 00:02:19.570
now you can have two
parts that are gonna hit,

00:02:19.570 --> 00:02:20.403
or all the pieces.

00:02:20.403 --> 00:02:21.236
What's gonna happen?

00:02:21.236 --> 00:02:22.930
So, a kinder, gentler
way is you go up there

00:02:22.930 --> 00:02:25.280
and you nudge it out of harm's way.

00:02:25.280 --> 00:02:26.760
If you get there early enough,

00:02:26.760 --> 00:02:28.810
you nudge it a little, that accumulates,

00:02:28.810 --> 00:02:30.640
and then it misses Earth entirely.

00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:31.567
- What are you nudging it with? Like a--

00:02:31.567 --> 00:02:34.033
- That's a good question! (laughs)

00:02:34.033 --> 00:02:35.269
- Uh, 'scuse me, uh.

00:02:35.269 --> 00:02:36.897
- An astronaut just goes up there

00:02:36.897 --> 00:02:37.918
and just goes, "That way, bro."

00:02:37.918 --> 00:02:39.842
(audience and guests laughing)

00:02:39.842 --> 00:02:42.724
- So, there are ways you
can do it gravitationally

00:02:42.724 --> 00:02:44.690
with what we call a gravitational tether.

00:02:44.690 --> 00:02:47.430
So, you have another object,
and they attract each other.

00:02:47.430 --> 00:02:49.080
And so, as they attract each other--

00:02:49.080 --> 00:02:49.913
- Like a magnet?

00:02:49.913 --> 00:02:51.890
- Well, with gravity, except, yeah.

00:02:51.890 --> 00:02:52.748
Gravity.

00:02:52.748 --> 00:02:53.581
- A gravity tether.

00:02:53.581 --> 00:02:54.414
- Gravity tether.
- A gravity tether,

00:02:54.414 --> 00:02:55.247
that's what I knew that.

00:02:55.247 --> 00:02:56.080
Why are-- You should explain it to her.

00:02:56.080 --> 00:02:57.580
I know.

00:02:57.580 --> 00:02:58.490
- So, they attract each other,

00:02:58.490 --> 00:03:00.310
but then this object you put up there

00:03:00.310 --> 00:03:02.020
has rockets in it pulls aside,

00:03:02.020 --> 00:03:03.680
and then they still attract each other.

00:03:03.680 --> 00:03:06.220
And it slowly drags it out of harm's way.

00:03:06.220 --> 00:03:07.210
That'll be a gravitational tractor.

00:03:07.210 --> 00:03:08.393
- So, that would have
been a much easier ending

00:03:08.393 --> 00:03:09.423
for all those movies.
- Like Armageddon!

00:03:09.423 --> 00:03:11.110
- Yeah, yeah, then you don't get

00:03:11.110 --> 00:03:12.876
the flood in New York and stuff like that.

00:03:12.876 --> 00:03:14.430
- Blowing it up would be fun, too.

00:03:14.430 --> 00:03:15.900
- So, here's the thing--
- Blow it up, yeah.

00:03:15.900 --> 00:03:18.190
- We know how to do it from
an engineering point of view,

00:03:18.190 --> 00:03:20.900
but there is no funded
project in the world

00:03:20.900 --> 00:03:22.180
to actually make that happen.

00:03:22.180 --> 00:03:24.130
So, we are just (slap) sitting ducks.

00:03:24.130 --> 00:03:26.058
- [Kelly] Oh my god!
- Do you know this?

00:03:26.058 --> 00:03:28.770
- That's comforting. Great.

00:03:28.770 --> 00:03:30.110
- So, we're all gonna die!

00:03:30.110 --> 00:03:32.781
(audience and guests laughing)

00:03:32.781 --> 00:03:33.614
- Totally kidding.

00:03:33.614 --> 00:03:35.369
- We need you to become engineers

00:03:35.369 --> 00:03:36.590
so you can solve this problem.

00:03:36.590 --> 00:03:37.904
- Yes, that's right!

00:03:37.904 --> 00:03:38.737
(clapping and cheering)

00:03:38.737 --> 00:03:39.570
- Work on the gravitational tether.

00:03:39.570 --> 00:03:43.810
Well, tell us about the
new season of Cosmos.

00:03:43.810 --> 00:03:46.290
- So, Cosmos, you know, it began in 1980

00:03:46.290 --> 00:03:47.707
with Carl Sagan (imitates Carl Sagan).

00:03:48.937 --> 00:03:51.993
"Billions--" I practiced
my "billions and billions."

00:03:51.993 --> 00:03:55.720
And I had the honor,
his widow and co-creator

00:03:55.720 --> 00:03:59.754
and co-writer, Ann
Druyan, invited me to host

00:03:59.754 --> 00:04:03.473
a revival of that in 2014 and then

00:04:03.473 --> 00:04:08.473
another season coming out 2020.

00:04:08.530 --> 00:04:13.530
And so, this is the
continuation of this effort

00:04:13.560 --> 00:04:16.030
to bring science to the public,

00:04:16.030 --> 00:04:18.360
not in a preachy way, not in a sit down,

00:04:18.360 --> 00:04:20.970
take your medicine, this is
science class, pay attention.

00:04:20.970 --> 00:04:21.803
No!

00:04:21.803 --> 00:04:23.790
In a way where you not only learn,

00:04:23.790 --> 00:04:26.740
but you absorb the science in a way

00:04:26.740 --> 00:04:28.150
that matters to you.

00:04:28.150 --> 00:04:30.810
Where you are compelled to action.

00:04:30.810 --> 00:04:31.876
Where you say--

00:04:31.876 --> 00:04:32.740
- Inspires.

00:04:32.740 --> 00:04:33.573
- It inspires!

00:04:33.573 --> 00:04:35.810
Wow, I never knew that
and it effects this,

00:04:35.810 --> 00:04:37.230
and our civilization depends on it.

00:04:37.230 --> 00:04:38.770
I'm all in.

00:04:38.770 --> 00:04:40.390
- And in that, we have to inspire them

00:04:40.390 --> 00:04:41.223
to be a part of that.

00:04:41.223 --> 00:04:42.896
(audience applauding)

00:04:42.896 --> 00:04:43.953
- Well, I love you!

00:04:43.953 --> 00:04:45.124
I love when you come on.

00:04:45.124 --> 00:04:46.400
- This is the second time on the show!

00:04:46.400 --> 00:04:47.289
- I know!

00:04:47.289 --> 00:04:49.259
- I feel like a regular!
- You liked it!

00:04:49.259 --> 00:04:50.092
You liked it!

00:04:50.092 --> 00:04:50.925
You came back!

00:04:50.925 --> 00:04:51.758
You came back!

00:04:51.758 --> 00:04:52.591
- Is this only your first time?

00:04:52.591 --> 00:04:54.640
- Only my first time!

00:04:54.640 --> 00:04:55.473
I'm a newbie here.

00:04:55.473 --> 00:04:56.306
- Just follow me.

00:04:56.306 --> 00:04:57.237
I'll teach you how to
come back a second time.

00:04:57.237 --> 00:04:59.003
- Teach me all your ways.

